        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots in exactly like its siblings and reads as a natural part of this codebase.\n\n- **Right unit.** Soft-duplicate detection is its own one-public-method function class (`AssignPossibleDuplicate.service(@Val Owner, OwnerRepository)`), wired as a single `Assign*` step between `assignHousehold` and `save` with `govern: [ transaction ]`. This mirrors `AssignNamesakeCount` and `AssignBulkSignupWarning` precisely \u2014 same signature, same \"runs after BuildOwner / before SaveOwner so findAll excludes the owner being created\" placement, same javadoc shape explaining the pipeline position.\n- **No duplication / no catch-all.** It isn't bolted onto `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique` (which is the hard-duplicate guard that throws) nor onto an existing `Assign*` class. The hard-duplicate check correctly stays a throwing guard and the soft check stays a non-throwing field-assigner \u2014 the right separation, since the spec requires the owner to still be created.\n- **The bespoke loop is idiomatic here.** It doesn't reuse `OwnerIdentity.key(...)` because this is a different match (lastName + postcode + *different* telephone), and `AssignNamesakeCount` establishes the precedent of a function doing its own `findAll` comparison loop. Lowest-id determinism is a reasonable, self-documented local choice.\n- **Additive supporting changes are consistent.** The two `Owner` fields with plain getters/setters, the four dialect schema columns, and the two `readOnly` OpenAPI properties all follow the `bulkSignupWarning` / `namesakeCount` precedent column-for-column and field-for-field.\n\nA maintainer of this project would find nothing surprising here."
